Two Sum II - Input Array Is Sorted

Intuition

Since the array is already sorted in non-decreasing order, we can exploit this structure using two pointers placed at opposite ends of the array. The sum of the values at these pointers gives a running total. If the total is less than the target, we need a larger value, so we increment the left pointer to move toward higher values. If the total is greater than the target, we need a smaller value, so we decrement the right pointer to move toward lower values. When the sum matches the target, the active pointers represent the required elements, which we then convert to 1-based indices as requested.

Approach

Initialize two pointers: left pointing to the start of the array (index 0) and right pointing to the end of the array (index numbers.size() - 1).

Enter a loop that continues as long as left is strictly less than right.

Compute the sum of the elements at the two pointers: current_sum = numbers[left] + numbers[right].

Compare current_sum with target:

If current_sum equals target, return the 1-indexed positions {left + 1, right + 1}.

If current_sum is less than target, increment left to increase the sum.

If current_sum is greater than target, decrement right to decrease the sum.

Code

class Solution {
public:
vector twoSum(vector& numbers, int target) {
int left = 0;
int right = numbers.size() - 1;
while (left < right) {
int current_sum = numbers[left] + numbers[right];
if (current_sum == target) {
return {left + 1, right + 1};
} else if (current_sum < target) {
left++;
} else {
right--;
}
}
return {};
}
};

Time Complexity

O(N), where N is the length of the array. In the worst case, each step moves either the left or right pointer, meaning each element is examined at most once until the target sum is found.

Space Complexity

O(1), because the algorithm only uses a constant amount of extra memory for two pointer tracking variables (left and right), adhering strictly to the space constraint of the problem.

Why the optimal solution is better than the brute-force approach

A brute-force approach tests every possible pair of elements using nested loops, which takes O(N^2) time complexity. Another alternative—using a hash map to store seen values—runs in O(N) time but requires O(N) extra space, violating the explicit constraint of using only constant extra space.

The optimal two-pointer solution takes full advantage of the pre-sorted input array. It narrows down the search space deterministically in O(N) time while using O(1) extra space, ensuring maximal computational efficiency without any memory allocation overhead.
